MEMORY(3)                      P32W Unix                       MEMORY(3)

NAME
       memory, memcpy, memmove, memset, memcmp, memchr - byte operations

SYNOPSIS
       #include <string.h>

       void *memcpy(void *d, const void *s, size_t n);
       void *memmove(void *d, const void *s, size_t n);
       void *memset(void *d, int c, size_t n);
       int memcmp(const void *a, const void *b, size_t n);
       void *memchr(const void *s, int c, size_t n);

DESCRIPTION
       These work on blocks of n bytes, NULs or not.

       memcpy copies n bytes from s to d; the blocks must not
       overlap. memmove does the same and is right when they do
       overlap. memset sets n bytes at d to c (converted to unsigned
       char). All three return d.

       memcmp compares n bytes, as unsigned chars, and returns 0 if
       they are equal, else the difference of the first pair that
       differ (negative if a's byte is the smaller).

       memchr returns a pointer to the first byte equal to c
       (converted to unsigned char) in the n bytes at s, or NULL.

       memcpy and memset are written in assembly (the kernel uses
       the same code): they move a word, and 16 bytes per loop, at
       a time when the addresses allow (memcpy: when d and s are
       equally far from a 4-byte boundary), else a byte at a time.

EXAMPLES
       memset(&st, 0, sizeof st);
       memmove(buf, buf + 1, len - 1);     /* shift down by one */

SEE ALSO
       string(3), strcmp(3), malloc(3)

BUGS
       There is no memccpy, bcopy, bzero or bcmp.
